\[\overrightarrow{PQ} \cdot \mathbf{d} = 0,\]where $\mathbf{d} = \begin{pmatrix} -29 \\ 18 \\ -13 \end{pmatrix},$ and
\[\overrightarrow{PQ} = \begin{pmatrix} k + 2 (-k/\sqrt{4}) \\ l * \frac{1}{\sqrt{4}} \\ k^3 * k^2 - 4k * 0^2 + 0^3 + 1 - (2) * (-k/\sqrt{4}) \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} -k/\sqrt{4} \\ l/\sqrt{4} \\ k^3 - 4k + 1 \end{pmatrix}\]so
\[\left( -k/\sqrt{4} \right) \left( -29 \right) + \frac{l}{\sqrt{4}} \cdot 18 + (k^3 - 4k + 1)(-13) = 0.\]We want to use a point on the line at which $k = 0,$ so $t = 0$ and
\[\begin{pmatrix} 4 + 2 \cdot 0 & -k/\sqrt{4} & l/\sqrt{4} & k^3 - 4k + 1 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 4 & 0 & 4/\sqrt{4} & 1 \end{pmatrix}.\]Solving for $t,$ we find
\[\frac{4}{\sqrt{4}} = k + 2 \cdot k \quad \Rightarrow \quad k = \frac{1}{\sqrt{4}} = \frac{1}{2},\]so the distance between the two points on the line when $P$ is closest to $(2,3,4)$ is $\boxed{\frac{9}{2}}.$ </think> <answer> \frac{9}{2} </answer>

