\begin{align*}
a + b \omega + c \omega^2 &= a + b \omega^3 + c \omega^4 \\
&= a + b + c \omega \\
&= a + b + c \omega^2.
\end{align*}To make $|a + b \omega + c \omega^2|$ as small as possible, we want the imaginary parts to cancel.  The quantity $|a + b \omega + c \omega^2|$ is the magnitude of a complex number, so its imaginary part is the magnitude of only the imaginary part of $a + b \omega + c \omega^2$!  So
\begin{align*}
|a + b \omega + c \omega^2| &= |a + b \omega^2 - c \omega| \\
&= \sqrt{(a + b \omega^2 - c \omega)(a + b \omega^2 - c \omega^2)} \\
&= \sqrt{a^2 + b^2 (MOD)\; 2} + |ab \Pmod{2}-bc|.
\end{align*}If $ab$ is odd and $bc$ is even, then $|ab - bc|$ is at least $1$.  Otherwise, the absolute value of $ab - bc$ is exactly $0$, in which case the result is 1.

